 "result": "Both classes are in `src/tessel/views.py`.\n\n**Turning the class into a view function**\n- `VerbView` (line 138) subclasses `View` (line 16).\n- `View.to_view_func(name, *class_args, **class_kwargs)` (line 86) is a classmethod that builds a plain function `view(**kwargs)`. It is registered with `app.attach_route_rule(..., view_func=CounterAPI.to_view_func(\"counter\"))`.\n- On each request, `view` creates a new instance (or reuses one if `init_every_request = False`) and calls `self.run_view(**kwargs)` through `active_app.ensure_sync`.\n- It attaches `view_class`, `methods`, `provide_automatic_options`, the name and the docstring to the function, and wraps it in any `decorators`.\n\n**Deciding which HTTP methods are allowed**\n- `VerbView.__init_subclass__` (line 165) runs when the class is defined. If the class doesn't set `methods` itself, it collects `methods` from base classes plus the uppercased name of each method in `http_method_funcs` that the class defines.\n- A class with `get` and `post` therefore ends up with `methods = {\"GET\", \"POST\"}`, and `to_view_func` copies that onto the view function so the router registers those methods.\n\n**Routing to the right handler**\n- `VerbView.run_view` (line 182) does `getattr(self, request.method.lower())`, falling back to `get` for `HEAD` when there's no `head` handler.\n- It asserts the handler exists, then calls it with the URL variables as keyword arguments, again through `ensure_sync`.",
